\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Card Game Rules\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 Overview\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The game uses a standard deck of 52 cards. The aim of the game is to place all of your cards into sets on the table, then end your turn by placing one card on the discard pile. It is loosely based on Gin Rummy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Setup\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Shuffle the deck and deal 9 cards to each player (the game is usually played with 2\'964 people). Place the remaining cards face down in the centre of the table \'97 this is the draw pile. Turn over one card and place it face up next to the draw pile \'97 this is the discard pile.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Turn Order\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The player to the left of the dealer takes the first turn, then play continues clockwise.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Starting and Ending a Turn\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 To 
\f0\b start
\f1\b0  a turn, a player must take either:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 One card
\f1\b0  from the draw pile, or\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 All cards
\f1\b0  from the discard pile.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 To 
\f0\b end
\f1\b0  a turn, a player must place exactly one card face up on the discard pile.\
A player 
\f0\b wins
\f1\b0  by placing all of their remaining cards into sets on the table and then ending their turn by placing one final card on the discard pile. You cannot win by placing all your cards into sets alone \'97 the winning move is always the discard.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Within a Turn\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 While it is their turn, a player may place cards from their hand onto the table in 
\f0\b sets
\f1\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Sets\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 A valid set is either:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A group of the same number
\f1\b0  \'97 e.g. the four 7s (one from each suit: Clubs, Hearts, Spades, Diamonds)\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A run of the same suit
\f1\b0  \'97 e.g. the 3, 4, 5, 6 of Hearts\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Runs 
\f0\b cannot loop
\f1\b0  around \'97 for example, Queen, King, Ace, 2 is not a valid run.\
A set requires a 
\f0\b minimum of three cards
\f1\b0 , with no maximum \'97 except for a player's 
\f0\b opening set
\f1\b0 , which must be a minimum of 
\f0\b four cards
\f1\b0  (see below).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 The Opening Set\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 A player's opening set is their entry into the game. Until a player has placed an opening set, they may not rearrange the table (see below).\
The 
\f0\b first set
\f1\b0  a player places must contain at least 
\f0\b four cards
\f1\b0 . This is their opening set. Once a player has placed their opening set, all future sets they place (within the same or later turns) require only three cards.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Aces\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Aces are 
\f0\b wild
\f1\b0  \'97 they can represent any card value and any suit. A few important rules apply:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 An ace 
\f0\b cannot
\f1\b0  represent a 1 or an Ace in a run \'97 so Ace, 2, 3, 4 is not a valid run.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 An ace 
\f0\b can
\f1\b0  represent a King.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 An ace does 
\f0\b not
\f1\b0  need to be formally declared \'97 it simply needs to make sense within the set. For example, in the run 3, 4, Ace, 6 of Clubs, the ace is understood to represent the 5.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A player with an opening set may pick up a set containing an ace and 
\f0\b replace the ace
\f1\b0  with a card that completes the set, freeing the wild ace for use elsewhere. For example: if a set reads 3 of Clubs, 4 of Clubs, Ace of Diamonds, 6 of Clubs, an active player may take up that set, replace the Ace with the 5 of Clubs, and keep the Ace to use in another set.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Rearranging the Table\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Once a player has placed their opening set, they may rearrange the table freely \'97 an unlimited number of times within their turn.\
Rearranging means a player may:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Pick up any sets from the table into their hand\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reform and place new sets on the table in any configuration\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Rules that always apply when rearranging:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Every card on the table 
\f0\b must
\f1\b0  belong to a valid set at all times. Any card that cannot be placed into a valid set must be taken into the active player's hand.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Any player who had an opening set 
\f0\b before
\f1\b0  the active player's turn began must still have an opening set at the end of that turn. The active player may replace another player's opening set with a different valid set of four, but they may 
\f0\b not
\f1\b0  leave that player with no opening set at all.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 If rearranging would make it impossible to leave every previously-opened player with an opening set, then 
\f0\b that move cannot be made
\f1\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Replenishing the Draw Pile\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 If the draw pile runs out during a turn, the active player simply continues their turn as normal \'97 they have already drawn their one card for the turn, so no further drawing is needed. At the 
\f0\b end
\f1\b0  of that turn, once the discard has been played, the discard pile is shuffled and placed face down to become the new draw pile. One card is then turned face up to begin the new discard pile.\
}